# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true, which checks text files out
# with CRLF line endings. CNAME must stay exactly 21 bytes: the domain
# and one \n, with no BOM and no trailing content. GitHub Pages reads
# this file as the source of truth for the custom domain, and a
# trailing \r makes it reject the name, which drops the site back to
# the github.io address and breaks every link to
# mohamedelhayyany.com until it is fixed and DNS settles.
#
# index.html, style.css and script.js are CRLF in the working tree.
# autocrlf round-trips those losslessly, so they are left alone rather
# than normalised: converting them here would be a 19,000 line
# whitespace change nobody asked for.
CNAME text eol=lf

# No line-ending conversion on binaries, whatever git guesses them to
# be. A stray \r inserted into a PNG or a TTF corrupts the file.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.pdf  binary
*.ttf  binary
*.ico  binary
